#Quotes tell Python that something is text and should be taken literally. Without quotes, Python treats a word as a variable name. Numbers don't need quotes because Python reads digits as numbers, and putting quotes around a number turns it into text that can't be used in math.
#input() always returns text, even if the person types a number. To do math or comparisons with it, I convert it with int() for whole numbers or float() for decimals. If I don't, I get errors like 'str' and 'int'.
#= stores a value in a variable (an instruction). == compares two values and gives back True or False (a question), and it's used in if conditions.
#print("bill") prints the text exactly as written. print(bill) looks up the variable bill and prints the value stored in it. If no variable named bill exists, Python gives a NameError.
#An f-string builds one piece of text and puts variables or calculations inside curly braces { }. Unlike commas, it adds no automatic spaces, so I control the exact output, and I can format numbers inside the braces, like {price:.2f}.

